/*!
@defgroup ewdg
@ingroup mod_system External Watchdog Monitor (EWDG)
@brief Independent external safety-output watchdog for the YTMicro SDK.

@details
The EWDG module provides a safety-oriented watchdog that monitors software
refresh activity and can also observe an external digital input. Unlike the
internal watchdog, the EWDG does not reset the CPU directly. Instead, it drives
an independent `EWDG_out` signal so external hardware can reset the board or
force the system into a safe state.

The SDK exposes the EWDG through a two-layer design:
- The **driver layer** (`ewdg_driver.h/.c`) provides instance-based startup,
  refresh, reset-like restore, input-pin query, and interrupt-mask APIs.
- The **hardware access layer** (`ewdg_hw_access.h`) provides inline helpers
  for refresh-key writes, register programming, and reset-value restoration.

@section ewdg_features Key Features
- Drives an independent external safety-output signal instead of directly
  resetting the MCU core.
- Supports windowed refresh with programmable `compareLow` and `compareHigh`
  boundaries.
- Supports optional watchdog interrupt generation before software takes
  additional action.
- Supports external `EWDG_in` participation with selectable active-high or
  active-low assertion behavior.
- Supports programmable clock prescaler and, on supported devices, selectable
  watchdog clock source.
- Uses a guarded two-key refresh sequence (`0xA8`, `0x6C`) to reduce the chance
  of accidental servicing.
- Provides instance-based public APIs through `EWDG_DRV_*`.

@section ewdg_modes Supported Modes

| Mode / Safety Path | Key Setting or API Path | Description |
|--------------------|-------------------------|-------------|
| Basic timeout monitoring | EWDG_DRV_Init() + EWDG_DRV_Refresh() | Software must refresh the watchdog inside the programmed service window. |
| Windowed refresh | `compareLow` / `compareHigh` | Refresh requests are accepted only inside the valid counter range. |
| External-input-assisted assertion | `assertLogic != EWDG_IN_ASSERT_DISABLED` | The external `EWDG_in` pin participates in the safety decision. |
| Interrupt-assisted handling | `interruptEnable = true` | Hardware can signal the shared watchdog interrupt so software can log or enter a safe state. |
| Optional clock-source selection | `clockSource` (if present) | Selects SIRC or the device-specific alternate source before enabling EWDG. |
| Reset-like restore | EWDG_DRV_DeInit() | Restores the register block to reset-like values and refreshes the service counter. |

@section ewdg_arch Module Architecture

The EWDG module is organized in a two-layer architecture:

@verbatim
+-----------------------------------------------+
|               Application Code                |
|          (calls EWDG_DRV_* functions)         |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|         EWDG Driver Layer (ewdg_driver.h/c)   |
|  - Instance-based startup and refresh APIs    |
|  - Window validation and input-mode query     |
|  - Resolves base address through EWDG_BASE_PTRS |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|       EWDG HW Access Layer (ewdg_hw_access.h) |
|  - Refresh-key write helper                   |
|  - Raw register access helpers                |
|  - Reset-like restore helper                  |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|            EWDG Peripheral Hardware           |
|        (CTRL, CLK_CTRL, CMPL, CMPH, SERV)     |
+-----------------------------------------------+
@endverbatim

@subsection ewdg_arch_driver Driver Layer (ewdg_driver.h)
Application code should normally use the `EWDG_DRV_*` functions. The driver
accepts a zero-based instance index, validates the requested service window,
programs the startup registers, and protects refresh and restore sequences from
interrupt latency.

@subsection ewdg_arch_hw HW Access Layer (ewdg_hw_access.h)
This internal layer provides direct register helpers for:
- **Service Sequence** - EWDG_Refresh()
- **Module State & Control** - EWDG_IsEnabled(), EWDG_SetControl(), EWDG_GetControl()
- **Window Configuration** - EWDG_SetCompareLow(), EWDG_SetCompareHigh()
- **Clock Configuration** - EWDG_SetPrescaler(), EWDG_SetClockSource()
- **Reset & Restore** - EWDG_DeInit()

@section ewdg_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection ewdg_config_struct ewdg_init_config_t Fields
| Field | Type | Description | Default From EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() |
|-------|------|-------------|------------------------------------------|
| clockSource | ewdg_clock_source_t | Watchdog clock source when the field is available on the device. | EWDG_SIRC_CLOCK |
| assertLogic | ewdg_in_assert_logic_t | External input-pin assert behavior. | EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ZERO |
| interruptEnable | bool | Enable EWDG interrupt generation. | false |
| prescaler | uint8_t | Prescaler field written to `CLK_CTRL`. | 255U |
| compareLow | uint16_t | Earliest counter value at which a refresh is accepted. | FEATURE_EWDG_CMPL_MIN_VALUE |
| compareHigh | uint16_t | Latest counter value at which a refresh is accepted. | FEATURE_EWDG_CMPH_MAX_VALUE |

@subsection ewdg_config_assert EWDG Input Assert Modes
| Value | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| EWDG_IN_ASSERT_DISABLED | Disable participation of the external input pin. |
| EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ZERO | Treat a logic-low level on `EWDG_in` as an assert request. |
| EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ONE | Treat a logic-high level on `EWDG_in` as an assert request. |

@subsection ewdg_config_features Device Feature Macros
| Macro / Definition | Description |
|--------------------|-------------|
| EWDG_INSTANCE_COUNT | Number of EWDG instances implemented by the device. |
| EWDG_BASE_PTRS | Base-address table used by the driver layer. |
| FEATURE_EWDG_KEY_FIRST_BYTE | First refresh key byte written to `SERV`. |
| FEATURE_EWDG_KEY_SECOND_BYTE | Second refresh key byte written to `SERV`. |
| FEATURE_EWDG_CMPL_MIN_VALUE | Minimum supported compare-low reset value. |
| FEATURE_EWDG_CMPH_MAX_VALUE | Maximum supported compare-high value accepted by the driver. |
| FEATURE_EWDG_SUPPORT_LPO_CLOCK | Indicates whether `EWDG_LPO_CLOCK` is available. |
| EWDG_CLK_CTRL_CLKSEL_MASK | Indicates that the device exposes the clock-source selector field. |

@section ewdg_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection ewdg_usage_basic Pattern 1: Initialize and Periodically Refresh the Watchdog
Initialize the EWDG with default settings and service it inside the main loop.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

ewdg_init_config_t ewdgConfig;
status_t status;

/* Enable the EWDG clock and configure EWDG pins before this call. */
EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&ewdgConfig);
status = EWDG_DRV_Init(INST_EWDG, &ewdgConfig);

if (status == STATUS_SUCCESS)
{
    for (;;)
    {
        /* Application work goes here. */
        EWDG_DRV_Refresh(INST_EWDG);
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection ewdg_usage_window Pattern 2: Program a Custom Refresh Window and External Input Mode
Use a narrower refresh window and let the external input assert on logic high.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

ewdg_init_config_t ewdgConfig;

EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig(&ewdgConfig);
#if defined(EWDG_CLK_CTRL_CLKSEL_MASK)
ewdgConfig.clockSource = EWDG_SIRC_CLOCK;
#endif
ewdgConfig.assertLogic = EWDG_IN_ASSERT_ON_LOGIC_ONE;
ewdgConfig.interruptEnable = true;
ewdgConfig.prescaler = 128U;
ewdgConfig.compareLow = 16U;
ewdgConfig.compareHigh = 254U;

if (EWDG_DRV_Init(INST_EWDG, &ewdgConfig) == STATUS_SUCCESS)
{
    for (;;)
    {
        /* Refresh only after the counter enters the valid service window. */
        EWDG_DRV_Refresh(INST_EWDG);
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection ewdg_usage_query Pattern 3: Query Input Behavior and Disable the Interrupt
Read back the configured input behavior and mask the interrupt if the
application switches to a polling-only safety strategy.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

ewdg_in_assert_logic_t inputMode;

inputMode = EWDG_DRV_GetInputPinAssertLogic(INST_EWDG);
if (inputMode != EWDG_IN_ASSERT_DISABLED)
{
    EWDG_DRV_DisableInterrupt(INST_EWDG);
}
@endcode

@subsection ewdg_usage_restore Pattern 4: Restore Reset-Like Register Values
Return the register block to its reset-like state during controlled shutdown or
test cleanup.

@code{.c}
#define INST_EWDG (0U)

EWDG_DRV_DeInit(INST_EWDG);
@endcode

@section ewdg_api API Quick Reference

@subsection ewdg_api_driver Driver Layer

@subsubsection ewdg_api_driver_init Initialization & De-initialization
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DRV_Init() | Validate the startup window, program the registers, and enable EWDG. |
| EWDG_DRV_GetDefaultConfig() | Populate an `ewdg_init_config_t` structure with default startup values. |
| EWDG_DRV_DeInit() | Restore the register block to reset-like values. |

@subsubsection ewdg_api_driver_service Watchdog Service
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DRV_Refresh() | Issue the two-key refresh sequence while interrupts are masked. |

@subsubsection ewdg_api_driver_ctrl Status & Interrupt Control
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DRV_GetInputPinAssertLogic() | Decode the configured external input-pin behavior. |
| EWDG_DRV_DisableInterrupt() | Clear the interrupt-enable bit in the control register image. |

@subsection ewdg_api_hw HW Access Layer

@subsubsection ewdg_api_hw_service Service Sequence
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_Refresh() | Write the two watchdog refresh key bytes directly to `SERV`. |

@subsubsection ewdg_api_hw_state Module State & Control
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_IsEnabled() | Read the watchdog enable bit from `CTRL`. |
| EWDG_SetControl() | Write a raw control-register image. |
| EWDG_GetControl() | Read the raw control-register image. |

@subsubsection ewdg_api_hw_window Window Configuration
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_SetCompareLow() | Program the lower refresh-window boundary. |
| EWDG_SetCompareHigh() | Program the upper refresh-window boundary. |

@subsubsection ewdg_api_hw_clock Clock Configuration
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_SetPrescaler() | Program the watchdog clock prescaler field. |
| EWDG_SetClockSource() | Program the optional clock-source selector. |

@subsubsection ewdg_api_hw_restore Reset & Restore
| Function | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| EWDG_DeInit() | Restore the register block to reset-like values and refresh the counter. |

@section ewdg_notes Important Notes

@subsection ewdg_notes_clock Clock and Pin Dependency
The EWDG driver does not enable clocks or configure pins internally. Enable the
peripheral clock and set up the `EWDG_out` / `EWDG_in` pins before calling
EWDG_DRV_Init().

@subsection ewdg_notes_window Refresh Window Rules
The driver rejects configurations where `compareHigh <= compareLow` or where
`compareHigh` exceeds `FEATURE_EWDG_CMPH_MAX_VALUE`. After initialization,
software must refresh the watchdog only while the internal counter is inside
the programmed window.

@subsection ewdg_notes_keys Refresh Key Sequence
Refreshing the watchdog requires two consecutive writes of
`FEATURE_EWDG_KEY_FIRST_BYTE` and `FEATURE_EWDG_KEY_SECOND_BYTE` to the `SERV`
register. The driver masks global interrupts around this sequence to reduce the
chance of timing violations between the writes.

@subsection ewdg_notes_writeonce Startup Register Programming
The EWDG is intended to be configured once after reset. Calling
EWDG_DRV_Init() on an already-enabled instance returns `STATUS_ERROR`.

@subsection ewdg_notes_irq Shared Interrupt Vector
When interrupt mode is enabled, the EWDG uses the shared watchdog interrupt
vector (`WDOG_EWDG_IRQn` on supported devices). The application must install
and enable the IRQ handler itself; the driver does not manage the NVIC.

@subsection ewdg_notes_device Device Variations
Some derivatives expose the optional `CLKSEL` field and support
`EWDG_LPO_CLOCK`, while others provide only the default SIRC path or a
different alternate source. Guard clock-source assignments with
`EWDG_CLK_CTRL_CLKSEL_MASK` or the relevant device feature macro.

@section ewdg_integration Integration Guide

@subsection ewdg_integration_units Compilation Units
Compile the following file into the application:

@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/ewdg/ewdg_driver.c
@endverbatim

@subsection ewdg_integration_include Include Path
Add the driver include directory to the toolchain include path:

@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
@endverbatim

@subsection ewdg_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile symbols are required beyond the active device selection.

@subsection ewdg_integration_deps Dependencies
The public driver depends on:
- `device_registers.h` for peripheral instance definitions and register macros
- `status.h` for `status_t`
- `interrupt_manager.h` for temporary global interrupt masking during refresh
  and restore sequences
*/
